xxm values need to be aligned by 16 bytes for you to use a 4 value struct
Every single precision float is 4 bytes.

const char* in fasm do not need [] because they are values passed directly
[] would dereference the address,
the function DrawText(const char* title) expects an address so no []

order of linux abi V:
rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx, r8, r9, r10, r11

Variadic function:
   A function that takes a variable number of arguements like printf and sprintf.
   Each one requires 16 byte alignment, for example:
updateGame:
.update_card_number:
   sub rsp, 8
   lea rdi, [text.message]
   lea rsi, [fmt_int]
   mov edx, [card_number]
   xor eax, eax
   call sprintf 
   add rsp, 8
   
   Every function call in fasm requires 8 bytes so this variadic function needs
   rsp to be 16-byte aligned before usage

Structs:
   Structs used in assembly are aligned by 8 bytes so in cases of 5 ints(20 bytes), it should be 24 bytes

In cases of function calls with stack:
   lea rdi, [spade_img]
   mov rsi, spade_path
   call LoadImage
   
   ; LoadTextureFromImage(Image image) - Image passed by value, returns Texture2D by hidden pointer
   lea rdi, [spade_tex]          ; hidden return pointer (rdi = where to store Texture2D)
   
   sub rsp, 32                   ; make room for Image struct on stack
   lea rax, [spade_img]          ; use rax as scratch — rdi is already set!
   mov rcx, [rax]
   mov [rsp], rcx
   mov rcx, [rax+8]
   mov [rsp+8], rcx
   mov rcx, [rax+16]
   mov [rsp+16], rcx
   
   call LoadTextureFromImage
   add rsp, 32

   Function (LoadTextureFromImage) is being called 8 bytes, 24 bytes for the image, 32 total.
